            {
              "id": "541ed21d-e25c-3962-944e-84fad2b8e44b",
              "name": "\"Rare Justification for Using @csrf_exempt in `index`\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `@csrf_exempt` decorator in Django is used to disable the cross-site request forgery protection on a particular view. In the given code, it is used on the `index` view. This can lead to a security vulnerability where an attacker can trick a victim into making a request that the victim did not intend to make.\n\nCross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) is an attack that tricks the victim into submitting a malicious request. It uses the identity and privileges of the victim to perform an undesired function on their behalf.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nThe use of `@csrf_exempt` should be avoided whenever possible. If it is necessary to use it, you should ensure that the view is safe from CSRF attacks. This can be done by checking the origin and referer headers to ensure that the request is coming from a trusted source.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nRemove the `@csrf_exempt` decorator from the `index` view:\n\n```python\ndef index(request):\n    latest_question_list = Question.objects.order_by('-pub_date')[:5]\n    context = { 'latest_question_list': latest_question_list, }\n    return render(request, 'polls/index.html', context)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django\n- Django's ORM\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP CSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross-Site_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-352: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-352",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-352"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5f6a2a58-a9e4-32d1-9ae2-76f5dad39bd7",
              "name": "Cross-Site Scripting Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the context of Python, particularly with Django framework, this vulnerability can occur if user input is directly rendered on the webpage without proper sanitization.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities in Python, particularly in Django, you should:\n\n- Always use Django's built-in templating engine which automatically escapes variables unless explicitly told not to. This helps prevent XSS attacks by ensuring that any user-supplied input is properly escaped before it is included in the output page.\n- Avoid using functions that can introduce XSS vulnerabilities such as `mark_safe`, `safe`, `unescape`, `serialize` etc. unless absolutely necessary.\n- Validate and sanitize user input before using it. This can be done using Django's form and model field validation.\n- Use Content Security Policy (CSP) headers to restrict the sources of scripts and other resources that can be loaded on your pages.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn your provided code, Django's templating engine is used which automatically escapes variables. So, it's already safe from XSS attacks unless you're using `mark_safe` or `safe` in your templates.\n\n```python\ncontext = { 'latest_question_list': latest_question_list, }\nreturn render(request, 'polls/index.html', context)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided is using Django, a high-level Python Web framework. So, Django is the only dependency required for the code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [OWASP Python Security - Cross-Site Scripting](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "19cc268b-fdb6-3a45-ba09-c963f54cb99e",
              "name": "Direct Data Rendering Bypassing Django's XSS Defenses Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nDirect Data Rendering Bypassing Django's XSS Defenses Vulnerability in Python refers to a security flaw where an application directly renders data without properly sanitizing it. This can lead to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks, where an attacker can inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. In this case, the vulnerability sink is the `HttpResponse` function, which is directly rendering the `question_id` without any sanitization or encoding.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always sanitize and validate any user-supplied data before rendering it. This can be done by using Django's built-in escaping functions, or by using a library such as Django's `bleach` to sanitize the data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the vulnerability sink:\n\n```python\nfrom django.utils.html import escape\n\ndef some_view(request, question_id):\n    response = \"Some response with %s\"\n    safe_question_id = escape(question_id)\n    return HttpResponse(response % safe_question_id)\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `escape` function from Django's `django.utils.html` module is used to sanitize the `question_id` before it is rendered.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
