            {
              "id": "faaac48b-e09e-3c39-906c-cfda2ad85f71",
              "name": "\"Rare Justification for Using @csrf_exempt in `detail`\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `@csrf_exempt` decorator in Django is used to disable the cross-site request forgery (CSRF) protection on a particular view. CSRF is an attack that tricks the victim into submitting a malicious request. It uses the identity and privileges of the victim to perform an undesired function on their behalf.\n\nIn the provided code, the `detail` view is exempted from CSRF protection. This means that if an attacker can trick a user into clicking a link or submitting a form, the attacker can perform actions with the same permissions as the user, without the user's knowledge or consent.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nThe use of `@csrf_exempt` should be avoided whenever possible, as it opens up potential security vulnerabilities. If you must use it, ensure that the view does not change the state of the system. If the view does change the state of the system, ensure that you have other protections in place, such as checking the HTTP `Referer` header.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nRemove the `@csrf_exempt` decorator from the view:\n\n```python\ndef detail(request, question_id):\n    question = get_object_or_404(Question, pk=question_id)\n    return render(request, 'polls/results.html', {'question': question})\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django\n- Django's `get_object_or_404` function\n- Django's `render` function\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP CSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross-Site_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-352: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
