            {
              "id": "01cd770c-4a42-3209-b0af-4a1cdd0ee379",
              "name": "High-Risk Dockerfile Vulnerability: Lack of Non-Root User Specification",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn Docker, a common vulnerability is the lack of specification of a non-root user in the Dockerfile. By default, Docker containers run as root. This means that if an attacker manages to break out of the application running inside the container, they would have root access to the host system, leading to a serious security breach.\n\nThis is a security concern because it gives potential attackers a high level of access to the host system if they manage to exploit a vulnerability in the application or the Docker daemon itself. This could lead to unauthorized access, data theft, or even a complete system takeover.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always specify a non-root user in the Dockerfile that will run the application. This can be done using the `USER` directive. By doing this, even if an attacker manages to break out of the application, they would only have the same permissions as the non-root user, significantly limiting the potential damage they could do.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to specify a non-root user in a Dockerfile:\n\n```Dockerfile\n# Create a group and user\nRUN addgroup -S appgroup && adduser -S appuser -G appgroup\n\n# Tell docker that all future commands should run as the appuser user\nUSER appuser\n```\n\nIn this example, a new group `appgroup` and user `appuser` are created, and then the `USER` directive is used to specify that all future commands should be run as `appuser`.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-250: Execution with Unnecessary Privileges](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/250.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-250",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/250.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-250"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d33d0e60-106e-322c-8774-0d2ba2e20c04",
              "name": "\"Dockerfile Vulnerability: Potential Exposure of Secret Environment Variables in ARG\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Dockerfile Vulnerability: Potential Exposure of Secret Environment Variables in ARG\" is a security vulnerability that arises when sensitive data, such as passwords, API keys, or other secrets, are exposed in the Dockerfile using the ARG instruction. In the provided code example, the superuser password for Django is being exposed:\n\n```Dockerfile\nARG DJANGO_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=adminpassword\n```\n\nThis is a security concern because anyone who has access to the Dockerfile can see the superuser password. This could lead to unauthorized access to the Django application, data breaches, and other security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the ARG instruction to store sensitive data. Instead, use Docker secrets or environment variables that are not committed with your code. These can be set at runtime, keeping them secure from unauthorized access.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using the ARG instruction, use an environment variable to store the superuser password. This can be done in the Dockerfile like so:\n\n```Dockerfile\nENV DJANGO_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD\n```\n\nThen, when running the Docker container, pass in the superuser password as an environment variable:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -e DJANGO_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=adminpassword ...\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible in the future."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-798",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "93ec716e-901d-3d62-9146-94f57845713d",
              "name": "\"High Severity Vulnerability in urllib3 Version 1.25.8, Resolved in Version 1.26.5 (CVE-2021-33503)\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability CVE-2021-33503 is a high severity vulnerability found in urllib3 version 1.25.8. This vulnerability allows a malicious actor to perform a Denial of Service (DoS) attack or possibly execute arbitrary code. The vulnerability is due to the way urllib3 handles certain HTTP responses. An attacker could exploit this vulnerability by sending a specially crafted HTTP response to an affected application, which could cause the application to crash or execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Security Concern\n\nThis vulnerability is a security concern because it allows an attacker to perform a DoS attack or execute arbitrary code. A DoS attack can make an application, service, or entire system unavailable to users. Arbitrary code execution can allow an attacker to take control of an affected system or application, potentially leading to data theft, data corruption, or further system compromise.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nThe recommended mitigation for this vulnerability is to upgrade urllib3 to version 1.26.5 or later, which contains a fix for this vulnerability. If upgrading is not immediately possible, a potential workaround is to add additional validation checks for HTTP responses in your application code.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nUpgrade urllib3 to version 1.26.5 or later by updating your requirements.txt file:\n\n```python\nurllib3==1.26.5\n```\n\nThen, run the following command to update the package:\n\n```bash\npip install -r requirements.txt\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- urllib3 version 1.26.5 or later\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 - A6:2017-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-20",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-20"
                ]
              }
