            {
              "id": "f6772744-842c-3274-81e4-6d921f7ab217",
              "name": "\"@csrf_exempt Usage Detected Indicating Potential CSRF Token Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `@csrf_exempt` decorator in Django is used to bypass the Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) protection for a particular view. In the provided code, the `inject` function is decorated with `@csrf_exempt`, which means that it does not require a CSRF token to be passed in the request. This can potentially lead to a CSRF attack, where an attacker tricks a victim into performing an action that the victim did not intend to do.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should remove the `@csrf_exempt` decorator to ensure that the CSRF protection is enabled for the view. If you need to disable CSRF protection for a specific reason, you should ensure that the view does not perform any state-changing operations and is safe to be accessed by an attacker.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```python\nfrom django.views.decorators.csrf import csrf_protect\n\n@csrf_protect\ndef inject(request, injector_str):\n    response = \"You're looking at the page that handles injection of %s\"\n    return render(request, 'polls/injector.html', {\n        'injector_str': unquote(injector_str),\n    })\n```\n\nIn this version, the `@csrf_protect` decorator is used to ensure that the CSRF protection is enabled for the view.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django: A high-level Python web framework.\n- urllib: A Python module for fetching URLs.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP CSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross-Site_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-352: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-352",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-352"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "637060ee-db68-331f-9a75-04bcd19e4c0c",
              "name": "Critical Dockerfile Vulnerability: Potential Exposure of Secret Environment Variables",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe Dockerfile vulnerability \"Potential Exposure of Secret Environment Variables\" is a security concern that arises when sensitive data, such as passwords, API keys, or other secrets, are exposed in Docker images. This can happen when environment variables are used in Dockerfiles to store such sensitive data. In the provided code example, the Django superuser password is stored in an environment variable:\n\n```Dockerfile\nENV DJANGO_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=${DJANGO_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD}\n```\n\nThis is a security concern because Docker images are often shared and can be inspected by anyone who has access to them. If the Dockerfile contains sensitive data, it can be extracted by anyone who can pull the image. This can lead to unauthorized access to systems, data breaches, and other security incidents.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid storing sensitive data in Dockerfiles or Docker images. Instead, use secure methods to handle secrets, such as Docker secrets, Kubernetes secrets, or environment variables that are set at runtime, not build time.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of setting the password in the Dockerfile, you can pass it as an environment variable at runtime. Remove the line from the Dockerfile:\n\n```Dockerfile\n# ENV DJANGO_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=${DJANGO_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD}\n```\n\nAnd when running the Docker container, pass the password as an environment variable:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -e DJANGO_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=your_password your_image\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example does not require any specific library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-798",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              }
