            {
              "id": "4b89bd9f-3874-34d3-974c-1c35e38d992f",
              "name": "'Sql_injector' function lacks rate-limiting decorator, potentially causing resource starvation due to high traffic volume.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `sql_injector` function in the provided Python code is vulnerable to SQL Injection attacks and Denial of Service (DoS) attacks due to lack of rate limiting. \n\nSQL Injection is a code injection technique that attackers can use to insert malicious SQL statements into input fields for execution. This can lead to unauthorized viewing of data, data manipulation, and data loss.\n\nDoS attacks can occur when an attacker sends a large number of requests to the function, causing resource starvation and potentially making the application unavailable to legitimate users.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate these vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n1. Use parameterized queries or prepared statements instead of string concatenation to prevent SQL Injection attacks.\n2. Implement rate limiting to prevent DoS attacks. This can be done using Django's `ratelimit` decorator.\n\n## Source Code Fix\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```python\nfrom django.views.decorators.csrf import csrf_exempt\nfrom django.views.decorators.ratelimit import ratelimit\nfrom django.db import connection\n\n@csrf_exempt\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')  # Adjust rate as needed\ndef sql_injector(request):\n    sql_str = request.GET.get('sql')\n    with connection.cursor() as cursor:\n        cursor.execute(\"SELECT * FROM my_table WHERE my_field = %s\", [sql_str])\n        sql_results = cursor.fetchall()\n    return render(request, 'polls/sequeleye.html', {\n        'sql_str': sql_str,\n        'sql_results': sql_results,\n    })\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following libraries to execute properly:\n\n- Django\n- django-ratelimit\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-770",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89",
                  "CWE-770"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "228f29c9-3627-3e05-94dc-b30e7bf3d2c4",
              "name": "\"SQL Injection Vulnerability Detected in Data Passed to execute() Function\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to SQL Injection, a code injection technique that attackers can use to exploit vulnerabilities in a web application's database layer. This happens when user input is either incorrectly filtered for string literal escape characters embedded in SQL statements or user input is not strongly typed and unexpectedly executed. In this case, the `execute()` function is directly using the input from the user, which is a dangerous practice as it allows an attacker to input malicious SQL queries.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate SQL Injection vulnerabilities, it is recommended to use parameterized queries or prepared statements. This ensures that user input is always treated as literal values and not part of the SQL command. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```python\nfrom django.db import connection\n\nuser_input = request.GET['sql']\ncursor = connection.cursor()\n\n# Use parameterized query to prevent SQL Injection\ncursor.execute(\"SELECT * FROM my_table WHERE my_field = %s\", [user_input])\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django: A high-level Python Web framework.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4047b11f-b4db-3de8-8932-a52713c7f1ef",
              "name": "\"Search Function Lacks Rate-Limiting Decorator Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Search Function Lacks Rate-Limiting Decorator Vulnerability\" in Python refers to a situation where a search function does not have a rate-limiting mechanism in place. This can lead to a potential Denial of Service (DoS) attack, where an attacker can send a large number of requests in a short period of time, causing the server to become overwhelmed and potentially crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement a rate-limiting mechanism on the search function. This can be done using Django's `ratelimit` decorator. This decorator allows you to specify a rate limit for a view. If the rate limit is exceeded, the decorator will raise a `Ratelimited` exception.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can modify the code to include a rate-limiting mechanism:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@csrf_exempt\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')  # limit to 10 requests per minute per IP\ndef search(request, ):\n    was_limited = getattr(request, 'limited', False)\n    if was_limited:\n        # handle rate limit exceeded\n        return HttpResponse('Too many requests', status=429)\n    search_str = request.POST.get('search_string', 'found nothing in search')\n    return render(request, 'polls/search_detail.html', {'search_string': search_str,})\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django\n- django-ratelimit\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
