            {
              "id": "d770ea6c-f20e-3971-9419-a5b9cd111d48",
              "name": "\"Rare Justification for Using @csrf_exempt in `redirect_to_polls`\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `@csrf_exempt` decorator in Django is used to bypass the Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) protection. The `redirect_to_polls` function in the provided code is decorated with `@csrf_exempt`, which means it is not protected against CSRF attacks. CSRF is an attack that tricks the victim into submitting a malicious request. It uses the identity and privileges of the victim to perform an undesired function on their behalf.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nThe use of `@csrf_exempt` should be avoided whenever possible, as it opens up potential security vulnerabilities. If you must use it, ensure that the view does not have any side effects and is safe to be accessed by CSRF attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nRemove the `@csrf_exempt` decorator to enable CSRF protection on the `redirect_to_polls` function:\n\n```python\ndef redirect_to_polls(request):\n    return HttpResponseRedirect('/polls')\n```\n\nIf you need to disable CSRF protection for a specific reason, consider using other methods that do not expose your application to CSRF attacks. For example, you could use Django's `csrf_protect` decorator on a per-view basis.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP CSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross-Site_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-352: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html)"
              },
